   146	## Gate results
   147	
   148	### Gate A — PASS
   149	
   150	- A1: the target claim embeds the exact translated function body and executes
   151	  it through module load, lookup, call, return, and pop.  The material body
   152	  mutant changes observable behavior and fails.
   153	- A2/A3: no proof rule skips control or mutates state.  The two dynamic
   154	  dispatch lemmas are value-only, exactly guarded restatements of existing
   155	  static equations.  Name binding remains the supplied semantics' lookup
   156	  through the exact callee/module/builtins scopes.
   157	- A4: definitional functions have exhaustive disjoint constructor cases or
   158	  unconditional equations with structural descent.  Projection use is guarded;
   159	  overlaps with fixed static dispatch agree.
   160	- A5: `["a"]` is a realizable witness.  The false-result K mutation is rejected
   161	  with the correct `"a"` residual.
   162	
   163	The Haskell backend cannot use `isStr(V)` alone to refine a symbolic `V:Val`
   164	to the `str(CS)` constructor; a fixed-only constructor-split invariant was
   165	therefore explored and produced the genuine stuck dynamic `set(V)` residual.
   166	The final guarded-total-projection construction is the Kit's sort-refinement
   167	idiom: it preserves the fixed value through the partial cast and restates only
   168	the already checked static equations.
   169	
   170	### Gate B — PASS
   171	
   172	- The input is an arbitrary finite `ValSeq` satisfying `allStrings`; there is
   173	  no bound on list or string length.
   174	- Distinctness is not required by the theorem, so the prompt's distinct-word
   175	  lists are included.
   176	- The result summary directly encodes maximum unique-character count and
   177	  lexicographically smallest tie-breaking, and the invariant threads that
   178	  summary through every loop iteration.
   179	- The empty-list return `""` is an explicit additional behavior, not a domain
   180	  exclusion.
   181	- The fixed model represents a string as an `IntSeq` and orders its integer
   182	  codes.  The proof covers every string value represented by that model.
   183	  CPython's full Unicode/runtime representation remains a model boundary, not
   184	  a theorem-side length or alphabet restriction.
   185	
   186	### Gate C — PASS
   187	
   188	Every named assumption and validation artifact is recorded below, all commands
   189	exist in `prove.sh`, all positive commands have current `#Top`/exit-0 results,
   190	and both negative probes have the expected nonzero results.
